Issue:        System Services Misconfiguration
- Certain Windows features or apps do not work
- Errors appear stating a service is not running.
- System functions fail after updates or software installs.
- Services stop unexpectedly or do not start at boot.

          Cause (Root cause or most likely cause):
- Required Windows services are disabled or set to the wrong startup type.
- Services were changed by updates, third-party software, or manual configuration.
- Dependency services are not running.
- Service account permissions are misconfigured.

          Steps / Resolution:
Check service status
- type services.msc
- Locate the service related to the issue.
- Check if the service is Running or Stopped.

[Screenshot1.png]

Verify startup type
- Right click the service -> Properties
- Set Startup type to:
  - Automatic (most system services)
-  Manual (services started when needed)

.[screenshot2.png]



Start or restart the service
- Right-click the service -> Start or Restart.

Check service dependencies
- Open service Properties -> Dependencies tab.
- Ensure required dependency services are running.

[screenshot2.png]


Dependencies Tab (Important Info)
Top box: Services this service depends on
- These must be running
- Check and start these first


Bottom box: Services that depend on this service
- These may stop if this service stops
- Don't troubleshoot this


Windows relies on background services to perform core system tasks. If a service is disabled, stopped, or misconfigured, the dependent service fails.
Correcting startup settings, restarting services, resolving dependencies, and repairing system files restores normal system functionality.



Reset service configuration 
- Use:Command Prompt or PowerShell as Administrator.
- Reconfigure startup type if needed (can be done in services.msc)
- Repair related system files using:
  sfc /scannow
  DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth

Restart the computer
- Ensures all service changes apply correctly at boot.

            Why It Happened 
- Windows relies on background services to perform system tasks.
- If a service is disabled, stopped, or misconfigured, related features fail.
- Correcting startup types, restarting services, and fixing dependencies restores proper system functionality.
